WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.360
African silhouettes, production markers, and grading.

2
00:00:03.710 --> 00:00:08.290
This is the video for module four of Advanced Tailoring and Pattern Making.

3
00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:15.090
You will draft iro and buba, kaftans and boubous, and calculate fabric before you buy.

4
00:00:16.340 --> 00:00:23.420
Then you turn one tested pattern into a production set, with markers that save fabric, and grade it across sizes.

5
00:00:24.670 --> 00:00:41.380
A kimono-cut buba is a T-shape, with the sleeve cut in one with the body. For example, Yetunde in Abeokuta drafts for a client with a 104 cm bust. She chooses 20 cm of ease, so each quarter is 31 cm at bust level.

6
00:00:42.630 --> 00:00:53.120
She checks the hip: 112 plus 8, divided by 4, needs 30 cm. Her 31 cm side line is enough, so the side seam runs straight.

7
00:00:54.370 --> 00:01:04.930
The client wants a closer sleeve, which can pull up and tear at the underarm. So Yetunde adds a bias-cut gusset of 10 cm by 10 cm at each underarm.

8
00:01:06.180 --> 00:01:16.130
For the iro, she adds a 4 cm waistband with a 25 cm front overlap and two hooks and bars, so it stays secure but still looks like a wrapper.

9
00:01:17.380 --> 00:01:22.990
She tests the buba in calico and asks the client to lift both arms and dance a few steps.

10
00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:41.090
A boubou is built from rectangles folded at the shoulder, spanning wrist to wrist. Check fabric width first. For example, Hadiza in Kano has a client with an arm span of 158 cm, but her fabric is only 116 cm wide.

11
00:01:42.340 --> 00:01:50.280
The shortfall is 42 cm, so she adds two symmetrical side panels, each 24 cm wide with seam allowance.

12
00:01:51.530 --> 00:02:03.370
The centre panel is 2 times 138, plus 3 cm hem at each end. That is 282 cm. The side panels fit side by side in one more length.

13
00:02:04.620 --> 00:02:11.780
So she needs 564 cm, about 5.64 m, plus some for facing and print matching.

14
00:02:13.030 --> 00:02:24.770
6 yards is only about 5.49 m, which is not enough, so she buys 7. One yard is 0.9144 m, so always convert carefully.

15
00:02:26.020 --> 00:02:40.930
A boubou is built from rectangles folded at the shoulder. Check fabric width first. For example, Hadiza in Kano has a client with a 158 cm arm span, but her fabric is 116 cm wide.

16
00:02:42.180 --> 00:02:51.710
Marker A, one skirt at a time, is 1.4 m long. That is 2.1 square metres, so the efficiency is about 71 percent.

17
00:02:52.960 --> 00:03:02.950
The centre panel is 2 times 138, plus 3 cm hem at each end: 282 cm. The side panels need one more length.

18
00:03:04.200 --> 00:03:10.000
That is 3.6 square metres for 3.0 of pattern, about 83 percent.

19
00:03:11.250 --> 00:03:23.690
Each skirt now uses 1.2 m instead of 1.4, so 20 skirts save 4 m. But only turn pieces on plain fabric with no nap. Never on a one-way print or velvet.

20
00:03:24.940 --> 00:03:39.210
A marker is the layout of all pieces on the fabric. For example, Rukayat in Ilorin cuts choir skirts. Each skirt's pieces total about 1.5 square metres, on 1.5 m wide fabric.

21
00:03:40.460 --> 00:03:51.230
Cut away from your body, and keep your other hand flat and well away from the cutting line. Start with a small number of layers, because the blade drifts and lower pieces come out bigger.

22
00:03:52.480 --> 00:03:55.320
And never leave blades where children can reach them.

23
00:03:56.570 --> 00:04:03.850
Rotary cutters and electric cloth cutters are very sharp. Close or guard the blade every time you put it down.

24
00:04:05.100 --> 00:04:12.690
Cut away from your body, with your other hand flat and well away from the line. Start with few layers, because the blade drifts.

25
00:04:13.940 --> 00:04:19.190
She grades length by 1 cm per size, so XL is 2 cm longer at the hem.

26
00:04:20.440 --> 00:04:27.440
She nests M, L and XL on one sheet from a fixed reference point, and checks the lines are evenly spaced.

27
00:04:28.690 --> 00:04:35.210
Then she sews the XL in calico on a willing client, because problems show most in the largest size.

28
00:04:36.460 --> 00:04:46.410
To remember. Ease and the underarm curve shape the buba. Check fabric width and convert yards carefully. Nest pieces only on fabric with no nap.

29
00:04:46.760 --> 00:04:50.250
Guard every blade, and test the largest graded size.

30
00:04:51.500 --> 00:04:58.930
Now draft a buba and iro, plan a boubou with its fabric, compare two markers, and grade one pattern three sizes.

31
00:04:59.280 --> 00:05:01.220
Continue with the lessons below.
